{
	title: 'Every transcript item has an id; web rows keyed by it',
	description: "Every history record carries an id: the session's next number (1, 2, 3, ...), written by the host, the one writer of a home's histories, so numbers never collide; elsewhere a record is named '<session id>#<n>'. A transcript item's id comes from its record (plus its index when one record yields several items, such as a prompt with images) and exists from the item's first streamed byte: the host numbers a record when its block starts streaming, and the saved record keeps that number, so a live item and the same item after reconnect, reload or a page of earlier history have the same id. Old histories without ids get them from their position when read. A client's optimistic item (a pending prompt) has a temporary id until the host's arrives, and the swap must not remount it. The web transcript keys its rows by item id (Solid's keyed <For>, never keyed={false}), and a card's open state is stored by item id, so loading earlier history or switching tabs never shows another item's card open (questions.md item 117). The 4s render test loads a page of earlier history and asserts the cards already shown stay connected and keep their open state.",
	status: 'done',
	needs: ['4s', 'bq', 'qn'],
	foldInto: ['7', 'ca', '4s'],
	notes: [
		"Records carry `n` (replay.ts HistoryRecord). history.number() takes the next one from max(state.next, pages marks.next); a streamed block takes its number when its first event arrives (Running.ns), so the file may hold records out of number order. Old records without `n` get byte offset + 1 when parsed (pages.parse, readSync's partial tail): unique and stable, and marks.next moves past them.",
		"Item keys: transcript.ts `Item = Shown & { key }`, key `<n>` or `<n>.<i>` for the i-th item of one record (prompt then images; a round's tool results); `~<position>` only for hand-built records without `n`. Tests that compare items to literals strip keys with `shown()` (host-fixture.test.ts / transcript.test.ts).",
		"Events carry the record number too: turn-start, prompt, command, output, tool-results, turn-end, question, and stream (the streaming block's number); snapshot.turn.ns numbers the live blocks. A new event that makes items must carry `n`, or live and reloaded keys differ.",
		"Pending prompt swap: turn-start and prompt events carry `command` (the submit's id); web view.ts keeps ViewState.sent (item key -> command id) and view.rows keys that row by the command id, the key the pending row had (view.withPending), so keyed <For> moves the same node. The host sends turn-start before the ack, so withPending drops a pending prompt already in the rows (else duplicate keys).",
		"Card open state lives in a module signal in Card.tsx keyed '<session>#<row key>', not in the DOM: <For> reuses nodes across a tab switch (same keys in two sessions), so DOM-held state would leak between tabs.",
		"Browser test (web.test.ts, 'earlier history loads above') needs a session in the tab list: use tabs.create(cwd), not sessions.create, or the page opens a new tab. It fails with keyed={false} and without the sent map."
	]
}
